---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import TextBlock from '../components/TextBlock.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const footerLinks = [
  { label: 'About', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Lead Magnet', href: '/eec/' },
  { label: 'LinkedIn', href: 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-malashenko/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Behavioral Email Sequences — First Action Fix"
  description="[PLACEHOLDER] Behavioral email sequences for AI SaaS — trigger-based, data-driven, built for conversion."
  theme="behavioral"
  activePage="behavioral"
>

  <!-- ===== HERO ===== -->
  <Hero
    headline="Behavioral Email Sequences for AI SaaS"
    subheadline="[PLACEHOLDER] Traditional drip sequences send emails on a timer. Behavioral sequences send emails based on what users actually do. The difference in conversion is not incremental."
  />

  <!-- ===== WHY BEHAVIORAL ===== -->
  <TextBlock heading="Why Behavioral, Not Timed" alt>
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER] Traditional 1-3-7 day sequences treat every user the same. Behavioral triggers respond to individual actions. For AI SaaS — where usage patterns vary wildly — timed sequences are guessing. Behavioral sequences are reacting.</p>
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER] The data advantage: what you learn from behavioral triggers compounds. Every sequence run improves the next. You're training your email system the same way you train your AI product.</p>
  </TextBlock>

  <!-- ===== WHAT IT TAKES ===== -->
  <TextBlock heading="What It Takes">
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER] The infrastructure reality — event tracking (Mixpanel, Amplitude), a capable email platform, and someone who understands the logic. Most AI SaaS companies have the tracking but don't use it for email.</p>
  </TextBlock>

  <!-- ===== THE THEORY ===== -->
  <TextBlock heading="The Theory" alt>
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER] 26+ different behavioral interruption types. How they map to user psychology. Why certain triggers (feature-discovery moments, usage drop-offs) are high-leverage points for email.</p>
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER] Traditional 1-3-7 sequences actually lose in some instances where behavioral triggers win. Not always better — but when they're better, they're dramatically better.</p>
  </TextBlock>

  <!-- ===== TWO PATHS ===== -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb-4">Two Ways Forward</h2>
      <div class="grid-2 reveal-stagger">
        <div class="card reveal">
          <h3>Build With Me</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">[PLACEHOLDER] What the done-with-you service looks like. You design the behavioral logic, map triggers to sequences, and build the system together. Outcome: a running behavioral email system tailored to their product.</p>
          <a href="https://calendly.com/YOURLINK" class="btn btn--primary mt-3">Book a Call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <h3>Build It Yourself</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">[PLACEHOLDER] The DIY product bundle. User map, user pains, behavior applications, product stack, pricing model, the theory, AI prompts, 26+ interruption types, 150+ examples, voice adapter prompt.</p>
          <p class="mt-2" style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted); font-style: italic;">Coming soon — pricing and access details will be available here.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== FREE RESOURCE ===== -->
  <TextBlock heading="Free Resource" alt>
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER] How to correctly configure tags and what metrics to track — what to optimize for scaling instead of collecting noise.</p>
    <a href="/" class="btn btn--outline mt-3">Learn More About Me</a>
  </TextBlock>

  <Footer links={footerLinks} />

</BaseLayout>
